{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>Ascend this mountain of Abarim, etc.</b> One must investigate why this pericope was juxtaposed here, and why Mount Nebo was called 'the mountain of Abarim,' and when Moses said, 'May Elohei ha-ruhot appoint a man over the congregation,' why he did not ask that his authority be bequeathed to his sons." "And what seems right to me to say regarding this is as follows. For above it was stated: 'And you shall transfer (וְהַעֲבַרְתֶּם) his inheritance to his daughter' — Rashi explained that the reason it says 'וְהַעֲבַרְתֶּם' is to teach you that anyone who does not leave a son to inherit him, the Holy One, blessed be He, is filled with wrath (עֶבְרָה) against him. And one must press: 'to inherit him' — why is that necessary? He should have said 'anyone who does not leave a son.' Rather, he wanted to say: even though he leaves a son, but the son is not worthy of inheriting him — that is, he is not worthy of inheriting his father's distinction in Torah and wisdom and authority — then the Holy One, blessed be He, is filled with wrath against him, for not having guided his son so that he would be worthy to fill his place. And whence does Rashi derive this? For from Zelophehad one cannot learn it, since he had no sons at all. Rather, he relied on the juxtaposition of the pericope 'Ascend this mountain of Abarim' — for this reason the Holy One, blessed be He, called its name 'the mountain of Abarim,' because upon it HaShem was filled with wrath (עֶבְרָה) against Moses for not having guided his sons so that they would be worthy to inherit him — his authority and his prophecy, which depend on the preparation of the recipient's capacity. And from this expression Moses understood that his glory would not descend to his sons after him. And Moses himself explained the matter thus, for he said (Deuteronomy 3:26–28): 'But HaShem was wrathful (וַיִּתְעַבֵּר) with me for your sakes,' etc., and immediately after it: 'And charge Joshua,' etc. — he indicated with a finger that this is the wrath (עֶבְרָה) for which HaShem became wrathful with him: that he would bequeath his authority to Joshua and not to his sons. And since he perceived that the matter was withheld, he said immediately: 'May Elohei ha-ruhot appoint,' etc. And see above, pericope Lekh Lekha (15:4), on the verse 'This one shall not be your heir.' And even though 'to inherit him' does not fit this manner in the case of Zelophehad, nevertheless he used this expression on account of Moses, as stated. And in the case of Zelophehad it may be that the expression 'וְהַעֲבַרְתֶּם' does not depart from its plain sense, and this is its explanation: if it had said 'and you shall give (וּנְתַתֶּם),' I would say that the name of the deceased would still be called upon his inheritance, for what difference does it make to me whether the inheritance is given to his son or to his daughter? But since it says 'וְהַעֲבַרְתֶּם,' infer from this that even though it is given to his daughter, nevertheless it is as though he transferred his inheritance from those who would issue from his loins to another tribe, and as though he did not merit that his seed should inherit the land. From this we learn that the Holy One, blessed be He, is filled with wrath against him, for he did not merit to establish his name upon his inheritance." "And there is yet another secret in this matter, drawn from the secret of formation, as the Sages said (Bava Batra 60a): 'If the man emits seed first, she gives birth to a female,' and the commentators said that the first seed is lost and the one that follows after remains viable, and a female is always born from the woman's seed. And if so, it is as though the daughter is not from his seed."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {1 "The citation '(טו ד)' appears to refer to Genesis 15:4 (pericope Lekh Lekha), rendered here as (15:4); the chapter number follows standard chapter divisions.", 2 "The Talmudic citation '(ברכות ס)' (Berakhot 60) does not correspond to the quoted teaching about seed and gender; the source for this statement is Niddah 31a. The text as given reads 'ברכות ס'; translated as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:43:29.980830422Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}